---
import { FAQSection, RelatedArticles } from "../../components/article";
import ArticleLayout from "../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";

const title = "Spain Digital Nomad Visa 2025: How to Apply";
const description =
  "Complete guide to Spain's digital nomad visa. Requirements, application process, timeline, costs, and tips from someone who actually did it successfully.";
const authorSlug = "daniel-brooks";
const pubDate = new Date("2025-11-24");
const readingTime = 14;
const image = "/images/unsplash/photo-1539037116277-4db20889f2d4-1200w.webp";
const imageAlt = "Barcelona Spain cityscape with Sagrada Familia";

const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: "introduction", text: "Why Spain" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "visa-overview", text: "The Visa Basics" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "eligibility", text: "Who Can Apply" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "required-documents", text: "Documents You Need" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "application-process", text: "How to Apply" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "costs-timeline", text: "Costs and Timeline" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "beckham-law", text: "Beckham Law Tax Benefits" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "best-cities", text: "Best Cities" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "cost-of-living", text: "Cost of Living" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "healthcare", text: "Healthcare" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "faq", text: "Questions" },
];

const faqData = [
  {
    question: "What's the minimum income requirement?",
    answer:
      "You need to show monthly income of at least two thousand three hundred thirty four euros. That's two hundred percent of Spain's minimum wage for individuals. This increases by seventy five percent of minimum wage roughly eight seventy five for each additional family member. Self-employed might need to show higher income.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I work for Spanish clients?",
    answer:
      "Yes but with limits. Up to twenty percent of your income can come from Spanish companies. The remaining eighty percent must come from non-Spanish clients or employers. This is more flexible than Portugal's visa.",
  },
  {
    question: "How long does the visa last?",
    answer:
      "Initial visa is valid for one year or up to three years for certain applicants. Renewable for up to two additional years. After five years you may apply for permanent residency.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I bring family?",
    answer:
      "Yes. Spouse and children and dependent parents can apply together with you or join through family reunification. Each family member adds to the income requirement.",
  },
  {
    question: "Do I qualify for Beckham Law?",
    answer:
      "Digital nomad visa holders can apply for Beckham Law status. That offers a flat twenty four percent tax rate on Spanish income up to six hundred thousand compared to progressive rates up to forty seven percent. You must not have been a Spanish tax resident in the previous five years.",
  },
  {
    question: "What if I lose my remote job?",
    answer:
      "Your visa status depends on continued remote work. If you lose your job find new remote employment or leave Spain before your visa expires. Consult an immigration lawyer for your specific situation.",
  },
];

const relatedArticles = [
  {
    title: "Portugal Digital Nomad Visa Guide",
    excerpt: "Compare Spain with Portugal's digital nomad program.",
    href: "/guides/digital-nomad-visa-portugal",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1555881400-74d7acaacd8b-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Lisbon Portugal cityscape",
  },
  {
    title: "Top Destinations for Digital Nomads",
    excerpt: "Explore more options for working remotely abroad.",
    href: "/blog/top-destinations-digital-nomads",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1488646953014-85cb44e25828-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "World travel destinations",
  },
  {
    title: "Best Banking for Digital Nomads",
    excerpt: "Set up your finances for international living.",
    href: "/blog/best-banking-digital-nomads",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1563013544-824ae1b704d3-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Digital banking on laptop",
  },
];
---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    authorSlug={authorSlug}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="Travel"
    categorySlug="guides/travel"
    headings={headings}
>
  <p class="lead">
    Spain launched their digital nomad visa back in twenty twenty three. Making it way easier for
    <a href="/guides/can-you-work-remotely-another-country">remote workers</a> to enjoy tapas and beaches and siestas
    legally. Combined with the famous Beckham
    Law tax stuff Spain offers one of Europe's most attractive setups for people who work remotely.
  </p>

  <h2 id="introduction">Why Spain</h2>

  <p>
    Spain has always attracted travelers and expats but the new nomad visa makes it particularly
    nice for remote workers.
  </p>

  <p>
    Climate. Over three hundred sunny days in southern regions. Mild winters. Lifestyle. Famous
    work-life balance with late dinners and vibrant social culture. Cost. Lower than Northern
    Europe especially outside Barcelona and Madrid. Infrastructure. <a href="/guides/internet-speed-remote-work">Good
    internet</a> and modern
    coworking and reliable transport. Tax benefits. Beckham Law offers a flat twenty four percent
    tax rate for qualifying nomads. Location. Gateway to Europe Africa and South America.
    Healthcare. Excellent public and private systems. Variety. Beach mountains cities islands
    all in one country.
  </p>

  <h2 id="visa-overview">The Visa Basics</h2>

  <p>
    <a href="https://www.boe.es/diario_boe/txt.php?id=BOE-A-2022-21739" target="_blank" rel="nofollow">Spain's Ley de
      Startups enacted December twenty twenty two</a> according to Spain's official state bulletin introduced the nomad
    visa as part
    of broader measures to attract international talent. Lets non-EU remote workers live and work
    legally in Spain.
  </p>

  <p>
    Main features. Initial visa for one year though some cases get up to three years. Renewable
    for additional two-year periods. Total stay up to five years then permanent residency becomes
    possible. Up to twenty percent of income can come from Spanish clients. Dependents can join
    the application. Free travel within Schengen. Eligible for Beckham Law at twenty four percent
    flat tax.
  </p>

  <h2 id="eligibility">Who Can Apply</h2>

  <h3>Income Requirements</h3>

  <p>
    Individual needs about two thousand three hundred thirty four monthly. That's two hundred
    percent of minimum wage. With spouse add around eight seventy five monthly. Per child add
    about two ninety two monthly each.
  </p>

  <h3>Work Requirements</h3>

  <p>
    Remote employee or self-employed or freelancer. At least eighty percent of income from
    non-Spanish sources. Working relationship with employer or clients for at least three months
    prior. For employees the company must exist for at least one year.
  </p>

  <h3>Other Requirements</h3>

  <p>
    Non-EU or EEA citizen. No criminal record. Valid health insurance covering Spain. Not have
    been legally resident in Spain in the past five years for Beckham Law eligibility.
  </p>

  <h2 id="required-documents">Documents You Need</h2>

  <h3>Identity and Travel</h3>

  <p>
    Valid passport with six plus months validity. Passport photos meeting Spanish requirements.
  </p>

  <h3>Employment Verification</h3>

  <p>
    Employment contract or <a href="/tools/best-invoicing-tools">freelance contracts</a>. Letter from employer
    confirming remote work
    arrangement. Proof company has existed for one plus year. Evidence of three plus month
    working relationship.
  </p>

  <h3>Financial Documents</h3>

  <p>
    <a href="/guides/multi-currency-banking-nomads">Bank statements</a> for three to six months. Proof of income meeting
    minimum requirements. Tax
    returns from home country.
  </p>

  <h3>Additional Requirements</h3>

  <p>
    Criminal background check apostilled. Health insurance valid in Spain. Proof of qualifications
    and professional experience. Completed application forms.
  </p>

  <p>
    Documents must be translated to Spanish by a sworn translator and apostilled. Requirements may
    vary by consulate so always confirm with your local Spanish consulate.
  </p>

  <h2 id="application-process">How to Apply</h2>

  <h3>Option One From Abroad</h3>

  <p>
    Step one. Gather documents. Takes two to four weeks. Collect everything plus translations and
    apostilles. Often the most time-consuming part.
  </p>

  <p>
    Step two. Apply at Spanish Consulate. Submit your application at the Spanish consulate in your
    home country. Book an appointment in advance because slots fill quickly.
  </p>

  <p>
    Step three. Wait for approval. Around twenty to thirty days typically. The consulate reviews
    your application. You might be asked for additional documents.
  </p>

  <p>
    Step four. Travel to Spain. Once approved enter Spain with your visa. You have ninety days to
    apply for your TIE which is the foreigner identity card.
  </p>

  <h3>Option Two From Within Spain</h3>

  <p>
    If you're already in Spain legally on tourist visa or whatever you can apply at the UGE in
    Madrid. That's the Large Companies and Strategic Collectives Unit. Often faster around twenty
    days but requires being physically present.
  </p>

  <h2 id="costs-timeline">Costs and Timeline</h2>

  <h3>Government Fees</h3>

  <p>
    Visa fee is around eighty euros. TIE card is about sixteen euros. Total official fees roughly
    one hundred euros.
  </p>

  <h3>Additional Costs</h3>

  <p>
    Sworn translations run fifty to two hundred. Apostilles run ten to fifty per document. Criminal
    record check runs twenty to fifty. <a href="/blog/best-travel-insurance-remote-workers">Health insurance</a> runs
    fifty to two hundred monthly.
    Immigration lawyer if you want one runs five hundred to two thousand.
  </p>

  <h3>Timeline</h3>

  <p>
    Document preparation takes two to four weeks. Visa processing takes twenty to thirty days. TIE
    appointment happens thirty to sixty days after arrival. Total from start to TIE is two to four
    months.
  </p>

  <h2 id="beckham-law">Beckham Law Tax Benefits</h2>

  <p>
    One of Spain's biggest draws. Named after footballer David Beckham who benefited from it. This
    special tax regime offers substantial savings for qualifying nomads.
  </p>

  <h3>How It Works</h3>

  <p>
    <a href="https://www.agenciatributaria.es/AEAT.internet/Inicio/_Segmentos_/Ciudadanos/Residentes/La_Sede_electronica_del_IRPF/Ayuda/_Informacion_general/Regimen_fiscal_especial_aplicable_a_los_trabajadores_desplazados_a_territorio_espanol__Ley_Beckham__.shtml"
       target="_blank" rel="nofollow">Flat tax rate of twenty four percent on Spanish income up to six hundred thousand
      euros</a> according to Spain's tax authority.
    Compare that to progressive rates up to forty seven percent. Available for the year of arrival
    plus five following years. Foreign income may be exempt from Spanish tax with some exceptions.
    Wealth tax only on Spanish assets.
  </p>

  <h3>Eligibility for Beckham Law</h3>

  <p>
    Not been a Spanish tax resident in the previous five years. Moved to Spain due to an employment
    contract or as a digital nomad visa holder. Must apply within six months of registering with
    Spanish Social Security.
  </p>

  <h3>Important Considerations</h3>

  <p>
    <a href="/blog/tax-tips-remote-workers">Consult a Spanish tax advisor</a> because rules are complex. Consider
    implications for your home
    country taxes. Beckham Law affects eligibility for some Spanish tax deductions. Rules may
    change so verify current regulations.
  </p>

  <h2 id="best-cities">Best Cities</h2>

  <h3>Barcelona</h3>

  <p>
    Spain's startup capital with a huge international community. Beach and culture and excellent
    coworking and vibrant nightlife and international airport. Cons are it's the most expensive
    Spanish city with tourist crowds and pickpocketing.
  </p>

  <h3>Madrid</h3>

  <p>
    Spain's capital and more Spanish than Barcelona. Great food scene and central location and
    lower costs than Barcelona and excellent transport. Cons are hot summers and no beach and
    less international feel.
  </p>

  <h3>Valencia</h3>

  <p>
    The sweet spot between Barcelona and smaller cities. Beach and affordable and growing tech
    scene and great weather and less touristy. Cons are smaller international community and
    fewer direct flights.
  </p>

  <h3>Canary Islands</h3>

  <p>
    Year-round spring weather on these Atlantic islands. Ideal climate and lower costs and nature
    and surf scene. Cons are isolation and limited nightlife and smaller communities.
  </p>

  <h3>Seville</h3>

  <p>
    Authentic Andalusian charm. Affordable and beautiful architecture and authentic Spanish culture
    and great food. Cons are extremely hot summers and smaller nomad community.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cost-of-living">Cost of Living</h2>

  <h3>Barcelona Monthly Budget</h3>

  <p>
    Rent for one-bedroom runs nine hundred to fifteen hundred. Coworking runs one fifty to three
    hundred. Groceries run two hundred to three hundred. Eating out runs two hundred to four
    hundred. Transport runs forty to eighty. Utilities run eighty to one fifty. Health insurance
    runs fifty to one fifty. Total around sixteen twenty to twenty eight eighty monthly.
  </p>

  <h3>Valencia and Other Cities</h3>

  <p>
    Expect twenty five to forty percent lower costs than Barcelona especially for rent. Comfortable
    budget in Valencia might be twelve hundred to eighteen hundred monthly.
  </p>

  <h2 id="healthcare">Healthcare</h2>

  <h3>Initial Health Insurance</h3>

  <p>
    For your visa application you need private health insurance with full coverage in Spain.
    Popular options include Sanitas and Adeslas and international policies like SafetyWing or
    Cigna Global.
  </p>

  <h3>Public Healthcare</h3>

  <p>
    Once you're a legal resident contributing to Social Security you can access Spain's excellent
    public healthcare system. Many nomads keep private insurance for faster access and
    English-speaking doctors.
  </p>

  <h2 id="faq">Questions</h2>

  <FAQSection faqs={faqData}/>

  <h2>Ready for Spain?</h2>

  <p>
    Spain's digital nomad visa combined with the Beckham Law tax benefits creates one of Europe's
    most attractive packages for remote workers. Whether you're drawn to Barcelona's beaches or
    Madrid's culture or the Canary Islands' eternal spring Spain has something for every type
    of nomad.
  </p>

  <p>
    Start your application early. Gather documents carefully. Consider professional help for tax
    stuff. Spain's bureaucracy can be slow but the lifestyle rewards are worth the patience.
  </p>

  <Fragment slot="full-width">
    <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
  </Fragment>
</ArticleLayout>
